   133	## 12. Conclusion
   134	
   135	**Observed effects (direct, matched, same GPU):** H faster than C3 at 1 s (+1.51 ms), 4 s (+0.85) and 6 s (+0.45); C3 faster at 8 s (−0.33, registered rule met with a thin margin over the 0.30 ms threshold). The 15 ms "C1P regression" is a build-order artefact of the matched runner (−15.26 with C1 first, −15.22 with C1P first; standalone C1P = C1). Freezing with inlining off gains +1.30 (8 s) / +0.74 (100 ms) on reduce-overhead, +1.31 / +1.03 on max-autotune, +1.36 / +1.03 on pad-and-batch; inlining off alone costs 0.2 / 0.08 ms. All new arms are numerically valid, fully graphed and pass the SDR gate.
   136	
   137	**Explanations supported by discriminating evidence:** the second-built arm's slow-down is GPU-busy time from an eager attention forward after dynamo's recompile limit is exhausted (logs in both orders, never standalone; profiled composition). Freezing's gain comes with the removal of the 420 parameter casts, rotary folding, norm+cast shrinkage and 413 fewer launches, and against a GEMM-class slow-down that is largest at 100 ms (−0.37 ms), which explains the base dependence at 100 ms.
   138	
   139	**Plausible but unverified:** that the launch-count reduction accounts for the gap between device-time deltas and wall gains (consistent with the Phase-3 per-node cost; not tested by intervention); that raising the recompile limit would remove the matched-runner artefact; that freezing's shift of GEMMs into Triton templates on the max-autotune base is what makes C3MG the fastest configuration (23.56 ms at 8 s, cross-session template counts).
   140	
   141	**Unresolved:** the location of any C3/H crossover between 6 s and 8 s (not sampled; no model); the 0.2 ms wall cost of inlining off, invisible in device time; why freezing slows the GEMM class (kernel selection with constant weights is the obvious suspect; not tested); the driver-version effect (confounded).
