docs/phase4/EXPERIMENT_REPORT_session4.md:31:- **Budget knob (owner-rule decision, recorded).** The registered plan set `SESSION_BUDGET_S=16200`; session-3 per-cell durations put the full workload near 4.8 h, so the session was launched with `SESSION_BUDGET_S=25200 EXTRACT_RESERVE_S=900` under the owner's standing instruction to raise the deadline rather than cut registered stages. Stage order, protocol, thresholds and stopping rules were unchanged; the session used 10,441 s.
docs/phase4/EXPERIMENT_REPORT_session4.md:36:Matched difference = first − second arm in ms (+ = second arm faster). Per repeat: median of 50 block latencies of arm A minus the same for arm B, blocks alternating in one process after a joint settle. Primary statistic: median over restarts of the per-restart median of the per-repeat differences. Interval: two-level cluster bootstrap (restart, then repeat; 10,000 resamples) of that same statistic (`fullmodel/stats_v2.py`); the historical pooled-repeat interval is also stored next to it but never used for a verdict. Eligibility: registered protocol 5×50, validation PASS at the arm's tier, joint settle affirmatively stable for two rounds, one session manifest, clock-flagged repeats excluded (≥ 1 clean repeat per restart required). Smallest meaningful difference: 0.30 ms at 4/6/8 s, 0.10 ms at 1 s and 100 ms. Verdict rule (plan line 21, implemented in `fullmodel/session4_result.py`): "second (or first) arm faster" when the interval excludes zero and |point| ≥ threshold; "practical tie" when the interval lies inside ±threshold; otherwise "unresolved". Three restarts limit every interval to the spread of three restart medians; that is stated, not hidden.
docs/phase4/EXPERIMENT_REPORT_session4.md:47:Reading. On this GPU, H (pad-and-batch) is faster than C3 at 1, 4 and 6 s by margins that clear the declared thresholds with room; at 8 s C3 is faster by 0.33 ms — the registered rule is met (interval excludes zero, |point| ≥ 0.30), while the interval [−0.39, −0.23] shows that the margin over the 0.30 ms threshold is thin: a fourth restart like the first (−0.297) could move the point estimate below it. All five A/A controls are practical ties (the 4 s A/A interval excludes zero but sits inside ±0.30 ms, which is what the tie rule measures). No crossover length is claimed: the grid has no point between 6 s and 8 s, the differences are not assumed linear, and nothing is refitted. The Sep-20 paired 8 s result (H slower by 0.495 ms on GPU-6bf4cb32), the Phase-3 4 s / 6 s pairs (GPU-7d47d5c2: H faster by 1.13 / 0.67 ms) and this session are three separately labelled observations; the direction at 4 s and 6 s replicates across the two sessions that measured them.
docs/phase4/EXPERIMENT_REPORT_session4.md:62:Mechanism, supported by the torch logs of this session in both build orders and absent from every standalone run: the two model instances share code objects, so the second build re-enters the first build's dynamo frames and adds guard-failure recompiles instead of tracing fresh frames; every C1-family build exhausts the recompile limit on the einops `rearrange` frame (`[3/8] torch._dynamo hit config.recompile_limit (8)`; part of the known 8-break structure), and only the second build in a matched process also exhausts it on the attention module's `forward` frame (`[7/8] … recompile_limit (8)`, attend.py:97; present in `torchlogs_matched_C1_C1P_352800_m*.log` and `torchlogs_matched_C1P_C1_352800_m*.log`, absent from `torchlogs_C1P_352800_r*.log` and the standalone trace logs), after which dynamo stops compiling that frame and the attention forward runs eager for the second-built arm. That accounts for the profiled composition (more, smaller kernels; fewer graph launches; more GPU time) and for the 5-vs-8 break count. The link limit → eager fallback → 15 ms is torch's documented behaviour and is recorded, but it was not tested by intervention (raising `torch._dynamo.config.recompile_limit` for the second build); that intervention is the smallest follow-up if the matched runner is ever used again for graph-breaking arms. Standalone unprofiled C1 was not part of the registered PB design (C1 was measured standalone in sessions 1–3); the result generator lists it as absent.
