58 Direct observation: the slow configuration follows the build position, not the arm. Standalone C1P equals standalone C1 (≈ 36.4–36.8 ms, 8 breaks, NOT_FULLY_GRAPHED as always for this family); whichever of C1/C1P is compiled second in a matched process compiles with 5 breaks instead of 8 and runs ≈ 15.2 ms slower. The Phase-3 statement "C1P is 15 ms slower than C1" is withdrawn as an arm property and restated as a matched-runner artefact for arms that compile with graph breaks; the Phase-3 records are unchanged and re-labelled ("compiled C1 vs partially compiled C1P"). C3-family arms show no such effect (A/A −0.009 ms at 8 s; identical break counts in both positions).
62 attend.py:97; present in `torchlogs_matched_C1_C1P_352800_m*.log` and `torchlogs_matched_C1P_C1_352800_m*.log`, absent from `torchlogs_C1P_352800_r*.log` and the standalone trace logs), after which dynamo stops compiling that frame and the attention forward runs eager for the second-built arm. That accounts for the profiled composition (more, smaller kernels; fewer graph launches; more GPU time) and for the 5-vs-8 break count. The link limit → eager fallback → 15 ms is torch's documented behaviour and is recorded, but it was not tested by intervention (raising `torch._dynamo.config.recompile_limit` for the second build); that intervention is the smallest follow-up if the matched runner is ever used again for graph-breaking arms. Standalone unprofiled C1 was not part of the registered PB design (C1 was measured standalone in sessions 1–3); the result generator lists it as absent.
